{
  "thought_process": "Analyzing Round 2 data with P=3.5, I=0.0, D=0.0. Setpoint is ~54.26. The time series clearly shows a sustained sinusoidal oscillation with no damping. The system swings from ~40.5 to ~59.5, giving a peak-to-peak amplitude of ~19, centered around ~50. The setpoint is 54.26, so the oscillation center (~50) is below setpoint, confirming a steady-state error of about 4.26 units. Overshoot is 9.7% which exceeds our 5% target. The oscillation has only 2 zero-crossings in this window but the pattern is clearly periodic with period ~2200ms (from min at ~51200ms to next min estimated). This is sustained oscillation - a classic sign that P=3.5 is too high and we are near or at the stability boundary. Comparing to Round 1 (P=2.0): Round 1 also showed oscillation. Now at P=3.5 we have 9.7% overshoot and sustained oscillation. We need to reduce P to damp the oscillation. The system is clearly oscillating with P=3.5. We should reduce P. However, we still have steady-state error (~1.46-4.26), which will eventually require I. But first we must stabilize the oscillation. A reduction to P=2.5 would be a moderate step back from 3.5, roughly 70% of current. This should reduce the oscillation amplitude. We cannot introduce I yet as the system is still oscillating - I would worsen instability. D could help dampen oscillations but per our methodology we should first fix P. Let me reduce P from 3.5 to 2.5 to try to get a damped response. This is a conservative reduction that should move us away from the oscillation boundary while still maintaining reasonable response speed.",
  "analysis_summary": "P=3.5 produces sustained sinusoidal oscillation with ~9.7% overshoot and steady-state error of ~1.46-4.26. The system swings symmetrically around ~50 (below setpoint 54.26) with period ~2.2s. This indicates P=3.5 is at or beyond the stability margin. Round 1 (P=2.0) also showed oscillation, suggesting the system is inherently prone to oscillation in this P range. Need to reduce P to obtain a damped response before proceeding. I and D remain at 0.",
  "tuning_action": "Reduce P from 3.5 to 2.5 to move away from sustained oscillation. This ~29% reduction should provide better damping while still maintaining reasonable response speed. Keep I=0 and D=0. If oscillation persists at P=2.5, further reduction will be needed. If response becomes overdamped, we can slightly increase P and then introduce a small I to handle steady-state error.",
  "p": 2.5,
  "i": 0.0,
  "d": 0.0,
  "status": "TUNING"
}

